    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 1971 Nissan Skyline 2000GT-R Is Now an 833-Piece Mattel Brick Shop Set That Can Be Customized\nVolvo Cutting 2,500 Additional Jobs Worldwide After Quarterly Revenue Fell 23% YoY\n22 photos\nPhoto: Volvo\nVolvo is cutting another 2,500 positions globally, consultants included. The Swedish automaker further relies on natural attrition and targeted exit packages to reach its target. Such a reduction is especially curious when you remember that Volvo still has plenty of new models to bring to market by 2030.\nThe majority of said reductions will fall in Sweden, with Volvo estimating 2,000 cuts in the automaker's home market. Add the new 2,500 target to the 3,000 positions announced earlier this year, and you get a whopping 5,500 layoffs. Make not mistake about it, Volvo is getting smaller because the current business isn't generating enough money.\nBased on the Q3 2026 numbers, the situation is ugly. Revenue fell a whopping 23 percent year over year, with Volvo confirming SEK 66.9 billion as opposed to 86.4 a year ago. That's USD 6.73 billion versus 8.69 a year ago. Given the aforementioned, operating income swung from a profit to a loss.\nEven if you exclude joint ventures, associates, and items affecting comparability, an operating loss of SEK 3.7 billion is not the same as the SEK 5.8 billion profit from a year ago. Its adjusted operating margin understandably contracted as well, falling from 6.7 to -5.6 percent.\nWanna talk cash? Okay! Per the attached Q3 2026 report, Volvo burned through SEK 8.9 billion in free cash flow during the quarter, which is more than twice the outflow from the same period last year. Polestar's Q2 2026 losses, unfavorable raw material costs, and a negative revaluation of US emission credits also hurt Volvo, and that's only part of the problem.\nPhoto: Volvo China is a tremendous headache for the company right now. Volvo reported that its revenue fell there nearly 50 percent year over year, citing intensified price competition and increasingly better Chinese alternatives. By protecting margins rather than chasing sales by matching every rival's discount, Volvo is losing precious market share in this incredibly lucrative market.\nA different squeeze came from the United States, where tariffs and the removal of consumer-facing incentives for electrified vehicles have definitely hurt profitability. Changes to legislation triggered the negative CAFE emission credit revaluation. Over in Europe, increasing demand for electric and electrified vehicles offered Volvo something to smile about.\nDespite said 5,500 layoffs, Volvo intends to launch all 13 models planned by the end of 2030. The mix is regionally tailored, with certain models developed specifically for Europe, the US, or China. Three of them are due in 2027, specifically two designed for China and one for Europe.\nAs for the EX60, which is currently hindered by manufacturing issues, the full-electric model has already secured 50,000 orders in Europe. Looking at the bigger picture, Volvo is going after more than office overhead. In addition to cutting positions worldwide, it's also tightening R&D and production costs.\nRegarding the latter, Volvo Cars' production will be concentrated around five dedicated plants. Multi-brand assembly with Geely is under consideration for the Ghent and Chengdu plants, and sharing more suppliers and parts with Geely should further reduce costs across the Swedish brand's model range.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolvo China US Europe EV\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Guy Has Been Working for Ford for 60 Years, Has Tools Older Than His Workmates",
